WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.147
These are the main ideas to remember.

00:00:02.147 --> 00:00:07.667
Normalize text deliberately and keep validation separate from transformation.

00:00:07.667 --> 00:00:10.352
Use string structure when parsing.

00:00:10.352 --> 00:00:13.307
Choose temporal types based on meaning.

00:00:13.307 --> 00:00:18.472
Remember that EXTRACT reads a component while DATE_TRUNC creates a time bucket.

00:00:18.472 --> 00:00:23.077
Use PostgreSQL interval arithmetic for elapsed and shifted time.

00:00:23.077 --> 00:00:28.262
And keep dates and timestamps typed until you truly need display text.

00:00:28.262 --> 00:00:34.157
Connect this idea to a CRM standardizing names, emails, and phone numbers.

00:00:34.157 --> 00:00:39.682
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:39.682 --> 00:00:47.287
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:47.287 --> 00:00:53.537
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:53.537 --> 00:00:59.680
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
